---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const img = (f: string) => `/articles/bytemark-cloud/${f}`;
---

<Base
  title="Bytemark Cloud backups"
  description="How a cross-company design sprint, a one-day prototype and usability testing turned into free backups for every Bytemark Cloud customer."
>
  <article class="wrap page">
    <header class="intro">
      <p class="crumbs"><a href="/work">Work</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> Bytemark Cloud</p>
      <h1>How backups came to Bytemark Cloud</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Designing, building and launching a backup feature for a cloud hosting product, while I was its product manager
        and led the product team, in 2016 and 2017.
      </p>
      <p class="edit-note">First published on the previous version of this site; lightly edited.</p>
    </header>

    <figure class="wide">
      <img src={img('how-might-we.jpg')} alt="A glass wall covered in sticky notes from the design sprint" width="864" height="397" loading="lazy" />
    </figure>

    <div class="body">
      <h2>Why backups</h2>
      <p>
        From regular customer interviews, the product team found that backups were both a consistent pain point and
        somewhere our cloud hosting product fell short. Customers without backups, or unsure whether they <em>really</em> had
        the backups they’d need in a crisis, were anxious about it. Some had found peace of mind with their own scripts, at the
        cost of upkeep and storage.
      </p>
      <blockquote>“I always have that bit of doubt whether the backup is actually running.”</blockquote>
      <p>Helping customers feel secure was too good an opportunity to miss.</p>

      <h2>A five-day design sprint</h2>
      <p>
        Early research told us a good backup service would be a real challenge for the company. To meet it, we brought people
        from across the organisation together for a five-day{' '}
        <a href="https://www.thesprintbook.com/">Google Ventures design sprint</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide">
      <img src={img('bytemark-cloud-sprint-roadmap.jpg')} alt="The sprint’s whiteboard map, from the long-term goal down to the customer journeys for backing up and restoring" width="864" height="644" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Day one: the long-term goal, “easy to implement and well-functioning backups and restores for everyone, by default”, and the map beneath it.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="body">
      <p>
        On day one we set the long-term goal, chose a narrow target of existing cloud customers, and picked two tasks to
        prototype: setting up backups, and restoring from one. By day four, after a run of group design exercises, we were
        ready to prototype and test.
      </p>

      <h2>Testing a prototype</h2>
      <p>
        We built the prototype in a day, inside the existing product, in HTML, CSS and JavaScript. We took shortcuts, and it
        still tested our coding and our nerve. Looking back I’d swap that for a prototyping tool, but putting the prototype
        inside a product the testers already knew did help them find their way.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="video">
      <video controls preload="none" poster={img('backup-prototype.jpg')} width="710" height="484" title="The backup prototype in use">
        <source src={img('backup-prototype.mp4')} type="video/mp4" />
      </video>
      <figcaption>The one-day prototype, as the testers saw it.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="body">
      <h2>Building a beta</h2>
      <p>
        From the usability research I sketched an interface that could be configured for complicated backup schedules, but
        kept the basic service simple enough that every customer could use it with almost no effort.
      </p>
      <blockquote>“It’s time-critical, so finding it in the panel is easier. It’s point and click.”</blockquote>
    </div>

    <div class="gallery">
      <figure>
        <img src={img('sketches.jpg')} alt="Paper sketches of the backup schedule settings" width="544" height="354" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Sketches for scheduling</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={img('prototype-screenshot3.png')} alt="The first backup panel in the prototype, with one schedule and a backup-now button" width="544" height="234" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>The prototype’s backup panel</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={img('prototype-screenshot4.png')} alt="The beta backup panel, with several schedules, their costs, and a list of manual backups to restore" width="544" height="536" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>The beta, with schedules and costs</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={img('clicktest.png')} alt="A click-test heatmap over the restore screen’s search and browse options" width="544" height="302" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>A click test on restoring a file</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src={img('terminal-prototype.jpg')} alt="A terminal session listing a server’s backups and mounting one to restore files" width="544" height="363" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Restoring from the command line</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="body">
      <h2>Easy backups for everyone, by default</h2>
      <p>
        It was a challenging project for Bytemark’s engineering team, and for me as a new product manager. We had to
        compromise on what we could deliver before it had proved its revenue.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’m proud of the pricing I worked out: fair to the customers who used it heavily, fair to the company’s investment,
        and with a free service included. By default, for every customer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-block: clamp(56px, 8vw, 96px) 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 40px;
  }

  .intro {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .crumbs {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  .crumbs span {
    margin-inline: 6px;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(32px, 4.4vw, 50px);
    line-height: 1.12;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: clamp(17px, 1.6vw, 19px);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .edit-note {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--faint);
    padding-left: 14px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--orange);
  }

  .body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1em;
    max-width: 680px;
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .body h2 {
    margin-top: 0.8em;
    font-size: 24px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .body a {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  blockquote {
    margin: 0.2em 0;
    padding-left: 18px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--line);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 19px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  figure {
    margin: 0;
  }

  figure img,
  figure video {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .wide {
    max-width: 864px;
  }

  .video {
    max-width: 710px;
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--faint);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .gallery {
    display: grid;
    max-width: 864px;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 340px), 1fr));
    gap: 28px 24px;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .gallery img {
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
</style>
